 Unknown or ambiguous targets must not be tested automatically.
 
+Scope rules must follow a grammar documented in an architecture
+decision record. A rule outside the grammar must be refused at
+registration. The first release supports only domain, wildcard, IP,
+and CIDR rules. A URL path, regular expression, unsupported scheme,
+or any other construct must be refused. Scope must never be widened to
+make an unsupported rule fit.
+
+Rules and targets are compared only in canonical form. A target that
+fails to parse, or has more than one possible canonical form, is
+ambiguous and must be denied. A target that no in-scope rule matches
+must be denied.
+
+Out-of-scope rules and exclusions take precedence over in-scope rules.
+
+A wildcard rule matches one or more labels below its base and never
+the base itself. A wildcard whose base is a public suffix, or cannot
+be confidently distinguished from one, must be refused at
+registration. A confirmation by the user alone must not make such a
+wildcard acceptable. Until a reviewed public suffix source is in
+place, every wildcard rule must be refused. No temporary or
+permissive wildcard handling is allowed.
+
+A rule without a port covers only ports 80 and 443. An exclusion
+without a port covers all ports. A redirect to a different port is a
+new target.
+
+A CIDR rule must be refused when its prefix is shorter than the minimum
+set by security policy. The default minimum is /24 for IPv4 and /48
+for IPv6. The minimum changes only through an explicit security policy
+change, never through AI output.
+
+Every address a target name resolves to, both A and AAAA results,
+must be evaluated. If any address is denied, the target is denied.
+
+The denied address list must be generated from the IANA
+special-purpose address registries and must add multicast addresses
+explicitly. It includes loopback, private, link-local, unique-local,
+multicast, reserved, and cloud metadata addresses. Addresses that are
+not globally routable unicast addresses must be denied. The cloud
+metadata addresses 169.254.169.254 and fd00:ec2::254 must always be
+denied.
+
+A denied address class is allowed only for an operation whose own
+target is a literal address, a CIDR range, or a hostname that a human
+registered as a lab target, with an audit event. The registration
+records the exact target and its kind, and it applies only to an
+operation whose own target is that exact target of that kind. This
+keeps explicitly registered local labs possible (see section 17). A
+public hostname that resolves into a registered lab range does not
+inherit the registration. Registering an ordinary hostname does not
+allow its address. A loopback lab registration must list its ports
+explicitly. Link-local, cloud metadata, and unspecified addresses must
+never be authorized. The application's own listeners and
+infrastructure endpoints (the API, the web server, the database, and
+any other endpoint the application runs or depends on) must never be
+authorized as testing targets. An explicit registration must not
+override this. A lab registration never relaxes process isolation
+(section 10).
+
+The connection must go to an address that passed evaluation. A later
+lookup must not change it. A name that resolves, through a CNAME or
+otherwise, into an excluded or non-public address must be denied.
+Every redirect must be treated as a new target and evaluated in full
+before it is followed. A redirect that cannot be fully evaluated must
+not be followed.
+
+A tool may reach the network only through an enforcement path that
+applies the rules of this section.
+
